# The fault catalog

A fault is something the author got wrong that Loom can name: a bracket left open, a label left empty, an anchor that resolves to no section. `LoomFault` is the closed set of them, and this module is the one place that spells out each fault's wording and severity.

Writing them once is the point. A reader sees everything Loom can say, gathered in one place. A detection site — in the tokeniser, the AST builder, or the product pass — names a fault and the span it covers, and never writes the words itself. `describe` turns a fault into the message a person reads, and `faulty` wraps that message, positioned, as the `Health` a node carries. Adding a fault is adding a line to the table. The table is exhaustive, so a fault with no line does not compile.

## What Loom can get wrong

A `LoomFault` is a tagged union, and each variant names one fault and carries only what its message needs. An `UnclosedDelimiter` carries the delimiter it expected to find. An `UnclosedFence` carries the fence that opened fenced code, so its message can show the line that would close it. A `FenceLabelAgainstLanguage` carries the label a fence wears and the language its section composes. An `UnresolvedAnchor` carries the name that matched no section, and a `Declaration` when the anchor's own loom is the place to declare that section: the text of a heading for it, and the point where the heading goes. It also carries `settleAt`, the point just past the anchor where a `{To be settled}` label can go. An `AmbiguousAnchor` adds the count that made it ambiguous and the `paths` that each name one of the candidates alone. A `ChapterWithoutCode` carries the name of a chapter that a code anchor composes, when the chapter's opening section holds no code to compose. A `CompositionCycle` carries the `loop` a composing anchor closes: the titles of the sections it passes through, from the anchor's own section back to that section again. Two notes in the catalog mark no mistake. An `UnsettledSection` carries the name a code anchor composes before its section holds any code, and whether a section of that name is declared yet. The section is to be settled: nothing is wrong while the author writes it. A `SettledLabel` carries the name of a section that holds code now, though the anchor still labels it `{To be settled}`, with the span of that label. A `DuplicateName` carries a name that two siblings share, the kind of sibling, and where the name already stands. Two sections of one loom are siblings, because a section's name is its identity within its loom. Two chapters of one part are siblings too, and so are two book looms that open one book. An `UnopenedBook` is a warning: it carries the name of a book that a `Book:` line names and no book loom opens. A `MissingWarpValue` carries the name of a Warp that bound nothing. An `UnknownVariable` carries the name a warp interpolated that the configuration never declared. A `CrossLanguageAnchor` carries the anchor name, the language found at the target, and the language the host section composes. A `HeadingAgainstCode` carries nothing either: it marks a heading written directly under a line of code, where a `#` comment most likely ended the code by mistake. A `DividerAgainstCode` is its twin for a line of dashes, which most likely belonged to the code, such as a YAML file's separator. A `FileInDiscussion` carries nothing: it marks a sink or a `{Tangle}` on a heading of a discussion, which writes no file. A `CodeInProse` carries nothing either: it marks an arrow in a section written in prose, which holds no code. A `CodeInConcept` is its twin for an arrow in a concept, and a `ConceptWithoutCode` carries the name of a concept that a code anchor names, since a concept holds no code to compose. An `UnresolvedLink` is a warning: it carries the name a link in prose writes when nothing is called that, no section, chapter, or form of a concept. A `ManifestOutsideTrope` carries nothing either: it marks a `{Manifest}` on a heading of a loom that names no trope. A `TropeRefused` carries the `reason` Loom refuses a trope's root for, such as a manifest that does not fit the contract, as Part XIII, Tropes, describes. A `ConclusionOutsideTopic` carries nothing either: it marks a conclusion anywhere but in a topic of a discussion. A `SecondConclusion` marks a conclusion in a topic that another already closed. A `StrayFragment` carries the text of a fragment a line had no place for, such as a second specifier on a heading. Four faults judge what the braces of an anchor or a box hold. A `RepeatedKey` carries a key written twice. A `LabelBesideParameter` carries a label written alone beside parameters, where only its full form is clear. An `UnknownKey` carries a key that the bearer of the braces does not take, with the `keys` it does take, and an `UnknownKind` carries a kind it does not take, with the `kinds` it does. Each of the two also carries `on`, which says whether the braces stand on an anchor or on a box, since the two take different keys and kinds. A `MissingSymbol` carries a name an anchor says its section provides, and the section that declares no such name. When a trope box promised the name, it carries the `trope` too. A `KeyOutsideTrope` carries a key that only a trope box takes, `provides` or `anchors`, written on a box of another kind. A `StrayTarget` carries a file an anchor writes in parentheses after its name, such as `(greeting.loom)`, when the anchor composes code, and the span it covers. Only a reference names a file, so the parentheses mean nothing on any other anchor.

Five faults judge a reference, an anchor of the `Reference` kind, as the chapter "Referring to modules" describes. A `NoSuchFile` carries the name a reference finds, the file it names in parentheses, the files the node it finds does emit, and the span of the parentheses. A `SeveralFiles` carries the name and the files, when the reference names none of them, with the point just past the anchor where a file can be named. A `SectionWithoutFile` carries the name of a section with no sink, the files it lands in, and whether one of them is the file the reference stands in, since a path can name none of those. An `UnsettledModule` is a note, like an `UnsettledSection`: it carries the name of a module nobody has written yet, and whether a chapter or section of that name is declared. A `SpecifierUnknown` carries the name of a reference whose path the package could not write, with the language and the reason. Three faults keep boxes whole, as the chapter "The tokeniser stage" describes. A `ColonRun` carries a run of four or more colons, which opens and closes nothing. A `NestedBox` carries nothing: it marks three colons that would open a box inside one already open. An `UnclosedBox` carries nothing either: it marks a box that runs to the end of its loom, because no line of three colons alone closes it. Five more judge a box that points somewhere: to a chapter, to a discussion, or, for a trope, to the section or chapter that holds it, as the chapter "The multi-file AST" describes. An `UnnamedBox` carries the box's `kind`, so its message can show a named box of that kind. An `UnwrittenLoom` is a note: it carries the kind and the name of a loom nobody has written yet, and the `opening` that writes it, a new loom's `file` and its `text`. An `UnsettledTrope` is a note too: it carries the name of a trope that points nowhere yet, and the `target` its `anchors` key names, when it names one. A `StaleSummary` carries what the box copies from, a chapter, a discussion, or a section, with its name, the `summary` the box should copy, and the span of the box's body, which no longer matches it. A `ChapterBoxBesideCode` carries the name of a chapter box written in a section that holds code. Two faults keep a heading to one mark for the file its section writes. A `TangleBesideSink` carries the sink a heading writes beside its `{Tangle}` specifier, and the span the fix removes. A `TangleWithoutFile` carries the folder a chapter's `Package` names, when it names one, since a `{Tangle}` specifier means the chapter's one file and a folder is no file.

Eight faults judge the boundary of a trope, as the chapter "The boundaries of a trope" describes. An `UnrootedTrope` is a note: it carries the name a `Rooted in:` line gives, when no trope chapter carries it. A `CircularRoots` carries the `loop` of tropes rooted in each other, from the trope whose root raises it back to that trope. An `EntriesOffRoot` carries nothing: it marks an `Entries:` line on a chapter that is no trope chapter. An `UnknownEntry` carries an entry's name that leads to no chapter. A `ForeignEntry` carries an entry's name that leads to a chapter of another trope, with that `trope`. A `PrivateEntry` carries an entry's name that leads to a chapter private to an inner `trope`. An `UnsettledMember` is a note too: it carries the trope whose chapter reaches a chapter that names no trope. A `ReachingInside` carries the `chapter` an anchor or a box reaches from outside its `trope`, and the `entries` that trope offers instead.

An `UnusedSection` carries the title of a section whose code no file includes, as the chapter "The product pass" describes.

The label faults fold many constructs into two variants. A construct is a named span the grammar validates — a specifier, a file path, a warp name, an anchor name. `EmptyLabel` is one with no text. `MalformedLabel` is one whose text breaks the construct's rule. Two further constructs — a warp annotation and a warp default — can only be empty, never malformed, so `EmptyConstruct` adds those two to `MalformedConstruct`.

=>

export type MalformedConstruct = 'specifier' | 'path' | 'warpName' | 'anchorName'

export type EmptyConstruct = MalformedConstruct | 'warpAnnotation' | 'warpDefault'

export type Sibling = 'section' | 'chapter' | 'book'

export type Declaration = { readonly at: Position; readonly text: string }

export type BracesOn = 'anchor' | 'box'

export type LoomFault = Data.TaggedEnum<{
  UnclosedDelimiter: { readonly expected: string }
  EmptyLabel: { readonly construct: EmptyConstruct }
  MalformedLabel: {
    readonly construct: MalformedConstruct
    readonly value: string
  }
  MissingWarpValue: { readonly name: string }
  UnknownVariable: { readonly name: string }
  UnresolvedAnchor: {
    readonly name: string
    readonly declaration?: Declaration
    readonly settleAt?: Position
  }
  AmbiguousAnchor: {
    readonly name: string
    readonly count: number
    readonly paths: ReadonlyArray<string>
  }
  ChapterWithoutCode: { readonly name: string }
  CompositionCycle: { readonly loop: ReadonlyArray<string> }
  UnsettledSection: { readonly name: string; readonly declared: boolean }
  SettledLabel: { readonly name: string; readonly label: Position }
  DuplicateName: { readonly name: string; readonly sibling: Sibling; readonly where: string }
  UnopenedBook: { readonly name: string }
  CrossLanguageAnchor: {
    readonly name: string
    readonly host: string
    readonly found: string
  }
  UnclosedFence: { readonly fence: string }
  FenceLabelAgainstLanguage: { readonly label: string; readonly language: string }
  StrayFragment: { readonly value: string }
  RepeatedKey: { readonly key: string }
  LabelBesideParameter: { readonly label: string }
  UnknownKey: { readonly key: string; readonly keys: ReadonlyArray<string>; readonly on: BracesOn }
  UnknownKind: {
    readonly kind: string
    readonly kinds: ReadonlyArray<string>
    readonly on: BracesOn
  }
  MissingSymbol: { readonly name: string; readonly section: string; readonly trope?: string }
  KeyOutsideTrope: { readonly key: string }
  StrayTarget: { readonly target: string; readonly at: Position }
  NoSuchFile: {
    readonly name: string
    readonly file: string
    readonly files: ReadonlyArray<string>
    readonly at: Position
  }
  SeveralFiles: { readonly name: string; readonly files: ReadonlyArray<string>; readonly at: Position }
  SectionWithoutFile: {
    readonly name: string
    readonly files: ReadonlyArray<string>
    readonly here: boolean
  }
  UnsettledModule: { readonly name: string; readonly declared: boolean }
  SpecifierUnknown: { readonly name: string; readonly language: string; readonly reason: string }
  TangleBesideSink: { readonly sink: string; readonly specifier: Position }
  TangleWithoutFile: { readonly folder?: string }
  ColonRun: { readonly colons: string }
  NestedBox: {}
  UnclosedBox: {}
  UnnamedBox: { readonly kind: PointingKind }
  ChapterBoxBesideCode: { readonly name: string }
  UnwrittenLoom: { readonly kind: LoomKind; readonly name: string; readonly opening: Opening }
  UnsettledTrope: { readonly name: string; readonly target?: string }
  StaleSummary: {
    readonly of: CopiedFrom
    readonly name: string
    readonly summary: string
    readonly at: Position
  }
  HeadingAgainstCode: {}
  DividerAgainstCode: {}
  FileInDiscussion: {}
  CodeInProse: {}
  CodeInConcept: {}
  ConceptWithoutCode: { readonly name: string }
  UnresolvedLink: { readonly name: string }
  ManifestOutsideTrope: {}
  TropeRefused: { readonly reason: string }
  UnrootedTrope: { readonly trope: string }
  CircularRoots: { readonly loop: ReadonlyArray<string> }
  EntriesOffRoot: {}
  UnknownEntry: { readonly name: string }
  ForeignEntry: { readonly name: string; readonly trope: string }
  PrivateEntry: { readonly name: string; readonly trope: string }
  UnsettledMember: { readonly trope: string }
  UnusedSection: { readonly name: string }
  ReachingInside: {
    readonly trope: string
    readonly chapter: string
    readonly entries: ReadonlyArray<string>
  }
  ConclusionOutsideTopic: {}
  SecondConclusion: {}
}>

~

Destructuring `taggedEnum` gives one constructor per variant, so a detection site builds a fault by name — `EmptyLabel({ construct: 'specifier' })`, or `MissingLanguageWarp()` for a variant that carries nothing.

=>

export const {
  UnclosedDelimiter,
  EmptyLabel,
  MalformedLabel,
  MissingWarpValue,
  UnknownVariable,
  UnresolvedAnchor,
  AmbiguousAnchor,
  ChapterWithoutCode,
  CompositionCycle,
  UnsettledSection,
  SettledLabel,
  DuplicateName,
  UnopenedBook,
  CrossLanguageAnchor,
  UnclosedFence,
  FenceLabelAgainstLanguage,
  StrayFragment,
  RepeatedKey,
  LabelBesideParameter,
  UnknownKey,
  UnknownKind,
  MissingSymbol,
  StrayTarget,
  NoSuchFile,
  SeveralFiles,
  SectionWithoutFile,
  UnsettledModule,
  SpecifierUnknown,
  TangleBesideSink,
  TangleWithoutFile,
  ColonRun,
  NestedBox,
  UnclosedBox,
  UnnamedBox,
  ChapterBoxBesideCode,
  UnwrittenLoom,
  UnsettledTrope,
  KeyOutsideTrope,
  StaleSummary,
  HeadingAgainstCode,
  DividerAgainstCode,
  FileInDiscussion,
  CodeInProse,
  CodeInConcept,
  ConceptWithoutCode,
  UnresolvedLink,
  ManifestOutsideTrope,
  TropeRefused,
  UnrootedTrope,
  CircularRoots,
  EntriesOffRoot,
  UnknownEntry,
  ForeignEntry,
  PrivateEntry,
  UnsettledMember,
  UnusedSection,
  ReachingInside,
  ConclusionOutsideTopic,
  SecondConclusion,
} = Data.taggedEnum<LoomFault>()

## The table

`describe` reads a fault aloud. Through `Match.valueTags`, it maps each variant's tag to a `severity` and the `message` a person sees, and it is exhaustive over the union — a new fault does not compile until it has a line here. The table cannot drift from the faults it describes.

A message is written for the author, who reads it beside the line it marks. It says plainly what is wrong, in one short sentence, and then what to do about it, when one fix is clear. It names things by the words the book defines, such as chapter, section, anchor, trope, and entry, and leaves Loom's own workings out of it. It opens on the problem itself, with no label before it to match the others. Every message in the table follows this rule.

A label's message depends on its construct. `empty` gives each construct the message for leaving it empty, with the fix that fills it. A malformed label reads `` `<value>` is not a valid <noun> ``, then states the rule its text broke: `noun` gives each construct that can be malformed the name a reader knows it by, and `rule` gives the rule. `withArticle` puts `a` or `an` before the noun, where it opens the second sentence.

`siblingPlace` names the other sibling that holds a duplicated name: another section of this loom, on the line it gives, another chapter of this part, in the looms it lists, or another book loom, in the looms it lists. `bearer` names what an unknown key or kind was written on, an anchor or a box, and `knownList` lists what that bearer takes, two joined by `and` and more by commas. `orList` joins names with `or`. A loop, of composition or of roots, reads as its names joined by arrows, which `loopLine` writes, and `elsewhere` names the other places that close it, every name between the first and the last.

A box that points somewhere needs a name, and its message shows one in an example. `exampleName` gives that name for each kind of such a box: `Rooms` for a chapter box, `Waking` for a discussion box, and `Payments` for a trope box. `copyWords` gives what a box copies from each thing it can copy, which `CopiedFrom` names: a chapter's opening, a discussion's summary, or a section's opening.

A reference's messages list files, and `fileList` joins them with backticks, as `` `a.ts` and `b.ts` `` reads. `emitted` says which files a node emits, or that it emits none of its own. `composeInstead` ends each message for a section with no file to name, since the author most likely meant the anchor to compose the section.

An ambiguous anchor ends its message with the way out. `disambiguation` names the paths that each reach one candidate alone. When there are none, as for two sections of one loom that share a title, it asks the author to rename one.

=>

type Note = { readonly severity: Severity; readonly message: string }

const error = (message: string): Note => ({ severity: 'error', message })

const warning = (message: string): Note => ({ severity: 'warning', message })

const information = (message: string): Note => ({ severity: 'info', message })

const cap = (text: string): string => text.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + text.slice(1)

const withArticle = (word: string): string => (/^[aeiou]/.test(word) ? `an ${word}` : `a ${word}`)

const empty: Record<EmptyConstruct, string> = {
  specifier: 'These braces are empty. Write a label in them, or remove them.',
  path: 'This sink names no file. Write a file name in its brackets, or remove them.',
  warpName: 'This warp has no name. Write one before its `=`.',
  anchorName: 'This anchor names nothing. Write the name of a section or chapter in its brackets.',
  warpAnnotation: 'This warp has an empty type. Write a type after its colon, or remove the colon.',
  warpDefault: 'This warp has no value. Write one after its `=`.',
}

const noun: Record<MalformedConstruct, string> = {
  specifier: 'label',
  path: 'file path',
  warpName: 'warp name',
  anchorName: 'anchor name',
}

const rule: Record<MalformedConstruct, string> = {
  specifier: 'may contain only words of letters, digits, hyphens, and underscores, one space apart',
  path: 'may contain only letters, digits, hyphen, underscore, dot, and slash',
  warpName: 'must be a TypeScript identifier',
  anchorName: 'may not contain `]`',
}

const bearer: Record<BracesOn, string> = { anchor: 'an anchor', box: 'a box' }

const exampleName: Record<PointingKind, string> = {
  Chapter: 'Rooms',
  Discussion: 'Waking',
  Trope: 'Payments',
}

const copyWords: Record<CopiedFrom, string> = {
  chapter: 'opening',
  discussion: 'summary',
  section: 'opening',
}

const loopLine = (loop: ReadonlyArray<string>): string =>
  Array.join(
    Array.map(loop, (title) => `\`${title}\``),
    ' → ',
  )

const elsewhere = (loop: ReadonlyArray<string>): string =>
  Array.join(
    Array.map(Array.dropRight(Array.drop(loop, 1), 1), (title) => `\`${title}\``),
    ' or ',
  )

const orList = (names: ReadonlyArray<string>): string =>
  Array.join(
    Array.map(names, (name) => `\`${name}\``),
    ' or ',
  )

const knownList = (known: ReadonlyArray<string>): string => {
  const marked = Array.map(known, (each) => `\`${each}\``)
  return marked.length > 2
    ? `${Array.join(Array.dropRight(marked, 1), ', ')}, and ${marked[marked.length - 1]}`
    : Array.join(marked, ' and ')
}

const siblingPlace: Record<Sibling, (where: string) => string> = {
  section: (line) => `Another section of this loom, on line ${line},`,
  chapter: (looms) => `Another chapter of this part, in ${looms},`,
  book: (looms) => `Another book loom, in ${looms},`,
}

const disambiguation = (paths: ReadonlyArray<string>): string =>
  Array.match(paths, {
    onEmpty: () => 'Rename one of them.',
    onNonEmpty: (all) =>
      `Write the path to the one you mean: ${Array.join(
        Array.map(all, (path) => `\`${path}\``),
        ' or ',
      )}.`,
  })

const fileList = (files: ReadonlyArray<string>): string =>
  Array.join(
    Array.map(files, (file) => `\`${file}\``),
    ' and ',
  )

const emitted = (files: ReadonlyArray<string>): string =>
  Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty(files) ? `It emits ${fileList(files)}.` : 'It emits no file of its own.'

const composeInstead =
  'Take `Reference` off the anchor and move it out of the import, so it composes the section.'

export const describe = (fault: LoomFault): Note =>
  Match.valueTags(fault, {
    UnclosedDelimiter: ({ expected }) =>
      error(`This is never closed. Add \`${expected}\` on the same line.`),
    EmptyLabel: ({ construct }) => error(empty[construct]),
    MalformedLabel: ({ construct, value }) =>
      error(
        `\`${value}\` is not a valid ${noun[construct]}. ${cap(withArticle(noun[construct]))} ${rule[construct]}.`,
      ),
    MissingWarpValue: ({ name }) =>
      error(`Warp \`${name}\` has no value. Give it one, as in \`${name} = …\`.`),
    UnknownVariable: ({ name }) =>
      error(
        `No variable is called \`${name}\`. Declare it under \`variables:\` in \`.loom/config.yaml\`.`,
      ),
    UnresolvedAnchor: ({ name }) =>
      error(
        `No section or chapter is called \`${name}\`. Write one, or label this anchor \`{To be settled}\`.`,
      ),
    AmbiguousAnchor: ({ name, count, paths }) =>
      error(`\`${name}\` names ${count} sections. ${disambiguation(paths)}`),
    ChapterWithoutCode: ({ name }) =>
      warning(
        `Chapter \`${name}\` holds no code in its opening section, so this anchor composes nothing. Name the section that holds the code instead.`,
      ),
    CompositionCycle: ({ loop }) =>
      error(
        loop.length > 2
          ? `Circular composition: ${loopLine(loop)}. Remove this anchor, or the one in ${elsewhere(loop)}.`
          : `Circular composition: \`${loop[0]}\` composes itself. Remove this anchor.`,
      ),
    UnsettledSection: ({ name, declared }) =>
      information(
        declared
          ? `\`${name}\` holds no code yet, so this anchor composes nothing.`
          : `No section is called \`${name}\` yet, so this anchor composes nothing.`,
      ),
    SettledLabel: ({ name }) =>
      information(`\`${name}\` holds code now. Remove the \`{To be settled}\` label.`),
    DuplicateName: ({ name, sibling, where }) =>
      error(`${siblingPlace[sibling](where)} is called \`${name}\` too. Rename one of them.`),
    UnopenedBook: ({ name }) =>
      warning(
        `No loom opens the book \`${name}\`. Write its book loom, a loom whose frontmatter holds only \`Book: ${name}\`.`,
      ),
    CrossLanguageAnchor: ({ name, host, found }) =>
      error(
        `\`${name}\` holds ${found} code, but this section holds ${host}. A section composes code of its own language only.`,
      ),
    StrayFragment: ({ value }) =>
      error(`This line has no place for \`${value}\`, so Loom would ignore it. Remove it.`),
    RepeatedKey: ({ key }) => error(`\`${key}\` is written twice in these braces. Keep one.`),
    LabelBesideParameter: ({ label }) =>
      error(
        `A label stands alone only in braces that hold nothing else. Write it in full here, as \`kind="${label}"\`.`,
      ),
    UnknownKey: ({ key, keys, on }) =>
      error(`${cap(bearer[on])} takes no \`${key}\` key. It takes ${knownList(keys)}.`),
    UnknownKind: ({ kind, kinds, on }) =>
      error(`${cap(bearer[on])} takes no \`${kind}\` kind. It takes ${knownList(kinds)}.`),
    MissingSymbol: ({ name, section, trope }) =>
      error(
        trope === undefined
          ? `\`${section}\` declares no \`${name}\`, though this anchor lists it in \`provides\`. Declare it in \`${section}\`, or take it out of \`provides\`.`
          : `\`${section}\` declares no \`${name}\`, though the \`${trope}\` trope promises it. Declare it in \`${section}\`, or take it out of \`provides\` on the trope box.`,
      ),
    StrayTarget: ({ target }) =>
      error(`Only a reference names a file, and this anchor composes code. Remove \`${target}\`.`),
    NoSuchFile: ({ name, file, files }) =>
      error(`\`${name}\` emits no \`${file}\`. ${emitted(files)}`),
    SeveralFiles: ({ name, files }) =>
      error(
        `\`${name}\` emits ${fileList(files)}. Name the one you mean in parentheses after its name, as \`(${files[0] ?? ''})\`.`,
      ),
    SectionWithoutFile: ({ name, files, here }) =>
      error(
        Match.value({ here, count: files.length }).pipe(
          Match.when(
            { here: true },
            () =>
              `\`${name}\` lands in the file this reference stands in, so no path can name it. ${composeInstead}`,
          ),
          Match.when(
            { count: 0 },
            () =>
              `\`${name}\` has no sink and lands in no file, so no path can name it. ${composeInstead}`,
          ),
          Match.orElse(
            () =>
              `\`${name}\` has no sink and lands in ${fileList(files)}, so no one path names it. ${composeInstead}`,
          ),
        ),
      ),
    UnsettledModule: ({ name, declared }) =>
      information(
        declared
          ? `\`${name}\` emits no file yet, so this reference names no module.`
          : `Nothing is called \`${name}\` yet, so this reference names no module.`,
      ),
    SpecifierUnknown: ({ name, language, reason }) =>
      error(`The ${language} package can't write the path to \`${name}\`. ${reason}`),
    TangleBesideSink: ({ sink }) =>
      error(
        `The sink \`${sink}\` already names the file this section writes. Remove \`{Tangle}\`.`,
      ),
    TangleWithoutFile: ({ folder }) =>
      error(
        `\`{Tangle}\` writes the file the chapter's \`Package\` names, but ${
          folder === undefined ? 'this chapter names no `Package`' : `\`${folder}\` is a folder`
        }. Name the file in brackets instead, as \`[module.ts]\`.`,
      ),
    HeadingAgainstCode: () =>
      warning(
        'This heading ends the code above it. If the line is a comment in the code, fence the block: three backticks on the line after its arrow, and three where the code ends. If it is a heading, leave a blank line above it.',
      ),
    DividerAgainstCode: () =>
      warning(
        'This divider ends the code above it. If the dashes belong to the code, format the loom to fence it. If the divider divides the chapter, leave a blank line above it.',
      ),
    ManifestOutsideTrope: () => error('Only a trope chapter can hold a manifest.'),
    TropeRefused: ({ reason }) => error(`Loom ignores this trope: ${reason}.`),
    UnrootedTrope: ({ trope }) => information(`No trope chapter is called \`${trope}\`.`),
    CircularRoots: ({ loop }) =>
      error(
        `Circular roots: ${loopLine(loop)}. Remove the \`Rooted in:\` line here, or the one in ${elsewhere(loop)}.`,
      ),
    EntriesOffRoot: () => error('Only a trope chapter can list entries.'),
    UnknownEntry: ({ name }) => error(`No chapter is called \`${name}\`.`),
    ForeignEntry: ({ name, trope }) =>
      error(`Chapter \`${name}\` belongs to the \`${trope}\` trope, not to this one.`),
    PrivateEntry: ({ name, trope }) =>
      error(
        `Chapter \`${name}\` is private to the \`${trope}\` trope, so this trope can't list it.`,
      ),
    UnsettledMember: ({ trope }) =>
      information(
        `Undeclared relation to the \`${trope}\` trope. Add \`Rooted in: ${trope}\` to its frontmatter.`,
      ),
    ReachingInside: ({ trope, chapter, entries }) =>
      error(
        Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty(entries)
          ? `Chapter \`${chapter}\` is private to the \`${trope}\` trope. Use it via ${orList(entries)} instead.`
          : `Chapter \`${chapter}\` is private to the \`${trope}\` trope, which has no entries yet.`,
      ),
    UnusedSection: ({ name }) => warning(`\`${name}\` is never used across the corpus.`),
    FileInDiscussion: () =>
      error(
        'A discussion writes no file, so it takes no sink or `{Tangle}`. Remove it, or move the section into a chapter.',
      ),
    CodeInProse: () =>
      error(
        'Prose holds no code. Quote this code in a fence, or name its language on the heading, as in `{TypeScript}`.',
      ),
    CodeInConcept: () =>
      error(
        'A concept holds no code. Quote this code in a fence, or move it to a section that realises the concept.',
      ),
    ConceptWithoutCode: ({ name }) =>
      error(
        `\`${name}\` is a concept, and a concept holds no code. Name the section whose code you mean.`,
      ),
    UnresolvedLink: ({ name }) =>
      warning(
        `Nothing is called \`${name}\`, so this link leads nowhere. Name a section, a chapter, or a form of a concept.`,
      ),
    ConclusionOutsideTopic: () =>
      error(
        'Only a topic holds a conclusion, and a divider opens each topic. Move this one below a divider, or make it a note.',
      ),
    SecondConclusion: () =>
      error(
        'This topic already has a conclusion. Merge the two, or open a new topic with a divider.',
      ),
    UnclosedFence: ({ fence }) =>
      error(
        `Nothing closes this fence, so its code runs to the end of the loom. Close it with \`${fence}\` on a line of its own, at the same column.`,
      ),
    ColonRun: ({ colons }) =>
      error(`A box line starts with three colons, not ${colons.length}. Write \`:::\`.`),
    NestedBox: () =>
      error(
        'A box never holds another box. Close the open one with `:::` on a line of its own, then open this one.',
      ),
    UnclosedBox: () =>
      error(
        'Nothing closes this box, so it runs to the end of the loom. Close it with `:::` on a line of its own.',
      ),
    UnnamedBox: ({ kind }) =>
      error(
        `This box needs a name. Write it before \`{${kind}}\`, as in \`::: ${exampleName[kind]} {${kind}}\`.`,
      ),
    ChapterBoxBesideCode: ({ name }) =>
      error(
        `This box begins the chapter \`${name}\`, so no code belongs in its section. Move the code into \`${name}\`, or give the box a section of its own.`,
      ),
    UnwrittenLoom: ({ kind, name }) =>
      information(
        `No ${kind.toLowerCase()} is titled \`${name}\` yet. Open it from this box, and the box's body becomes its opening.`,
      ),
    UnsettledTrope: ({ name, target }) =>
      information(
        target === undefined
          ? `The \`${name}\` trope points nowhere yet. Name the section or chapter that holds it with \`anchors\`.`
          : `No single section or chapter is called \`${target}\`, so the \`${name}\` trope points nowhere yet.`,
      ),
    KeyOutsideTrope: ({ key }) => error(`Only a trope box takes the \`${key}\` key.`),
    StaleSummary: ({ of, name }) => {
      const copy = copyWords[of]
      return warning(
        `This box no longer matches the ${copy} of the ${of} \`${name}\`. Copy that ${copy} into it, or run \`loom refresh\`.`,
      )
    },
    FenceLabelAgainstLanguage: ({ label, language }) =>
      error(
        `This fence is labelled \`${label}\`, but its section's code is ${language}. Label it \`${language}\`, or leave the label off.`,
      ),
  })

## Raising a fault

A detection site holds a fault and the span it covers, and needs a `Health` for the node. `faulty` is that step: it describes the fault through the catalog and returns a health carrying the one diagnostic, positioned where the fault was found. The severity sets the node's status: an error or a warning fails it. An informational note leaves it healthy, and the catalog raises two, for a section to be settled and for a label that outlived its section's settling. A tangle refuses an unsettled section all the same, as the chapter "The compile layer" describes, because a file that composes one would ship without the code its anchor names.

`fixesOf` gives the fixes a fault offers. An ambiguous anchor offers one for each of its paths, which writes the path in place of the anchor's name. An unresolved anchor offers up to two. One writes the heading of its declaration at the point the declaration names, so the section exists and the author writes its specification next. The other writes `{To be settled}` just past the anchor, so the section can wait without any prose. An anchor that states what its section provides has promised the section already, so it raises no unresolved-anchor fault and offers neither fix. A settled label offers one fix, which removes the label. A label beside parameters offers one fix too, which writes the label in full. A stray target offers one fix, which removes the target, and a `{Tangle}` beside a sink offers one, which removes the specifier. A run of too many colons offers one, which writes three in its place. A chapter or a discussion nobody has written offers one, which writes its loom, and a summary out of step offers one, which copies the loom's summary into the box. A reference that names no file, or one the node does not emit, offers one fix for each file the node emits, which writes that file in the parentheses. `diagnosticOf` builds the diagnostic, and gives it `fixes` only when there are some, so a fault with nothing to offer carries no empty list. `faultAt` gives one fault's diagnostic on its own, for a site that hands a diagnostic on rather than a node's health, as the compiler hands the editor a missing symbol to raise.

=>

const statusOf = (severity: Severity): Health['status'] => (severity === 'info' ? 'ok' : severity)

export const fixesOf = (fault: LoomFault): ReadonlyArray<Fix> =>
  Match.value(fault).pipe(
    Match.tag('AmbiguousAnchor', ({ paths }) =>
      Array.map(paths, (path): Fix => ({ title: `Write the path \`${path}\``, text: path })),
    ),
    Match.tag('UnresolvedAnchor', ({ name, declaration, settleAt }) => [
      ...Option.toArray(
        Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(declaration), (declared): Fix => ({
          title: `Declare the section \`${name}\``,
          text: declared.text,
          at: declared.at,
        })),
      ),
      ...Option.toArray(
        Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(settleAt), (at): Fix => ({
          title: `Mark \`${name}\` to be settled`,
          text: `{${settleKind}}`,
          at,
        })),
      ),
    ]),
    Match.tag('SettledLabel', ({ label }): ReadonlyArray<Fix> => [
      { title: 'Remove the `{To be settled}` label', text: '', at: label },
    ]),
    Match.tag('LabelBesideParameter', ({ label }): ReadonlyArray<Fix> => [
      { title: `Write \`${label}\` in full`, text: `kind="${label}"` },
    ]),
    Match.tag('StrayTarget', ({ target, at }): ReadonlyArray<Fix> => [
      { title: `Remove \`${target}\``, text: '', at },
    ]),
    Match.tag('NoSuchFile', ({ files, at }) =>
      Array.map(files, (file): Fix => ({ title: `Name \`${file}\``, text: `(${file})`, at })),
    ),
    Match.tag('SeveralFiles', ({ files, at }) =>
      Array.map(files, (file): Fix => ({ title: `Name \`${file}\``, text: `(${file})`, at })),
    ),
    Match.tag('TangleBesideSink', ({ specifier }): ReadonlyArray<Fix> => [
      { title: 'Remove `{Tangle}`', text: '', at: specifier },
    ]),
    Match.tag('ColonRun', (): ReadonlyArray<Fix> => [{ title: 'Write `:::`', text: ':::' }]),
    Match.tag('UnwrittenLoom', ({ kind, name, opening }): ReadonlyArray<Fix> => [
      {
        title: `Open the ${kind.toLowerCase()} \`${name}\``,
        text: opening.text,
        file: opening.file,
      },
    ]),
    Match.tag('StaleSummary', ({ of, name, summary, at }): ReadonlyArray<Fix> => [
      { title: `Copy the ${copyWords[of]} of \`${name}\``, text: summary, at },
    ]),
    Match.orElse((): ReadonlyArray<Fix> => []),
  )

const diagnosticOf = (note: Note, position: Position, fixes: ReadonlyArray<Fix>): Diagnostic =>
  Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty(fixes)
    ? { message: note.message, position, severity: note.severity, fixes }
    : { message: note.message, position, severity: note.severity }

export const faultAt = (fault: LoomFault, position: Position): Diagnostic =>
  diagnosticOf(describe(fault), position, fixesOf(fault))

export const faulty = (fault: LoomFault, position: Position): Health => ({
  status: statusOf(describe(fault).severity),
  diagnostics: [faultAt(fault, position)],
})

## What this module draws on

`Data` builds the tagged union and a constructor for each fault, `Match` builds the exhaustive table, `Array` turns an ambiguous anchor's paths into its message and its fixes, and the keys and kinds that an anchor or a box takes into the messages of an unknown key and an unknown kind, and `Option` reads an unresolved anchor's declaration and the point where it can be settled. `@athrio/loom-ast` types what a fault becomes — the `Diagnostic` with its `Severity`, `Position`, and each `Fix` it offers, and the `Health` a node holds — and gives `settleKind`, the kind the settling fix writes, the `LoomKind` and `PointingKind` a box that points somewhere names, `Opening`, the new loom an unwritten loom's fix writes, and `CopiedFrom`, what a box copies from.

=>

import { Array, Data, Match, Option } from 'effect'
import type { Diagnostic, Fix, Health, Position, Severity } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomNode'
import { type CopiedFrom, type Opening } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomCorpusAst'
import { type LoomKind, type PointingKind, settleKind } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomTokens'

